    "segments": [
      {
        "start": 1854.04,
        "end": 1872.02,
        "text": "Do less than you think you can do. This applies to any new habits, as far as I'm concerned. If you think you can do 20 minutes, but that's pushing it into redline territory, do 10. Do less than you think you can do because that is going to contribute to endurance and longevity and enthusiasm."
      },
      {
        "start": 3855.31,
        "end": 3877.97,
        "text": "People are like, I'm anxious. I'm like, how much coffee do you drink? I like three or five cups. I'm like, that's liquid anxiety. Of course you're anxious. So coming back to the fuel thing. So maybe it's not a problem solving problem. Yeah. Maybe it's just a biological issue. Yeah. Yeah. Caffeine is not a fuel. It's forcing you to use your fuel faster."
      },
      {
        "start": 6041.13,
        "end": 6067.93,
        "text": "But Peter Drucker, paraphrasing, effectiveness is doing the right things, and efficiency is doing things right. What you do matters more than how you get there. If you start in Kansas intending to reach California but point toward New York, speed does not matter. You are going the wrong direction."
      },
      {
        "start": 6456.05,
        "end": 6484.43,
        "text": "So the cost to constantly improve yourself is that you constantly look for ways to fix yourself, which means looking for ways you are broken. There is always one more problem, one more workshop, one more book, one more retreat. You are always going to be one step behind."
      }
    ]
  },
